Add one history comment for each metadata command.

Currently comments are only created for status changes. Generalize this behavior across all
metadata types. If one command changes multiple metadata items, put all generated change lines in
one comment, in command argument order:

```
2026-08-13 - David Reid

status changed from review to open.
assignee changed from David Reid to Clanker.
```

Use these messages for added, changed, and removed metadata:

```
priority set to high.
assignee changed from David Reid to Clanker.
milestone removed (was 1.1.0).
```

Do not generate a line for an unchanged value. If all requested values are unchanged and there is
no user message, do not add a comment.

Add `-m <text>`, `--message <text>`, `-F <path>`, and `--file <path>` options to both `set` and
`clear`. The message should follow generated change lines in the same comment block, separated by
one empty line:

```
2026-08-13 - David Reid

status changed from open to review.
assignee changed from Clanker to David Reid.

Ready for review.
```

The `-m` and `-F` forms must be mutually exclusive, must not occur more than once, and must contain
a non-empty message. Options should be accepted after metadata arguments and validated clearly.

The `--no-comment` option should suppress all generated metadata-change lines. A supplied `-m` or
`-F` message must still create a comment. This allows a user message to replace generated history
text without creating a separate comment section.

The complete operation must remain atomic. File input, arguments, metadata changes, comment data,
and allocations must succeed before the ticket file is replaced.


